      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Amlodipine 20 mg once daily is twice the FDA-labeled maximum of 10 mg per day. Nothing in the patient details you gave (70 kg, normal kidney and liver function, no allergies) would justify going above that. At higher doses you mostly get more edema, hypotension, and reflex tachycardia, with little added blood pressure benefit.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Start at the usual dose.** The usual adult dose is 5 mg by mouth once daily, and you can titrate to 10 mg after 1\u20132 weeks. For a frail or elderly patient, or one with hepatic impairment, you can start at 2.5 mg.\n- **Enter 10 mg once daily**, the maximum dose, if the patient already takes that.\n- **Add a second agent** if 10 mg isn't controlling blood pressure. Common choices are an ACE inhibitor or ARB, or a thiazide-like diuretic.\n\nIf 20 mg was a typo, tell me the dose you meant and I'll enter it. If you want 20 mg on purpose, for example because the patient is on it as an outpatient or your attending asked for it, please confirm that with your attending or pharmacist first. Then tell me, and I can enter it with a note recording that."
      }
     ],
